\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Path holonomy intertwines vertex gauge actions}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For a \vocabk{coarse path map}{fdip-00MO},
#{\mathcal B} is continuous, #{r:K_f\to K_c} is a continuous
surjective homomorphism, and
##{\mathcal B(g\cdot U)=r(g)\cdot\mathcal B(U).}
The image of normalized Haar under #{r} is normalized Haar on
#{K_c}. The measures #{\nu_f} and #{\rho=\mathcal B_*\nu_f}
are invariant under their respective gauge groups. Inverse
pullback satisfies #{R_gR_h=R_{gh}}.}

\proof{
\p{Continuity follows from continuity of multiplication and
inversion and the finiteness of every path. Let its successive
vertices be #{v_0,\ldots,v_\ell}. For either direction of an
edge traversal, its transformed transport is
##{g_{v_j}U_{e_j}^{\epsilon_j}g_{v_{j-1}}^{-1}.}
In the rightmost-first product, every adjacent
#{g_{v_j}^{-1}g_{v_j}} cancels. The remaining factors are
#{g_{v_\ell}(\mathcal BU)_ag_{v_0}^{-1}}, which is the
claimed endpoint action. The empty-path case gives
#{g_{v_0}eg_{v_0}^{-1}=e}.}

\p{Any coarse vertex transformation extends to the fine
vertices by assigning the identity at the other vertices.
Thus #{r} is surjective. Its Haar pushforward is a regular
probability invariant under every coarse left translation:
lift that translation to #{K_f} and use Haar invariance.
Normalized uniqueness in \ref{fdip-007B} identifies it with
coarse gauge Haar.}

\p{For each fixed #{g}, the edge action consists of left and
right translations. It preserves product Haar by
\ref{fdip-007B}; for the general compact Hausdorff case this
can be checked on continuous product functions and extended
by their uniform density and regularity. Equivariance and a
lift of each coarse transformation then show that #{\rho}
is invariant. Finally,
#{R_gR_hF(U)=F(h^{-1}g^{-1}\cdot U)=R_{gh}F(U)}.
This is the pathwise extension of the gauge transport
calculation in \ref{fdip-006E}.}
}
